Hello, thanks for your response. Prior to your message, I messed around with the `takeoff_async` and noticed that it can still fail/ throw errors but at least it doesn't throw a seg-fault (so you can handle the cases where this happens):

This code snippet is a little weird in implementation because I am turning an async method into a blocking one but this works and "gets the job done" so to speak. This one doesn't throw errors because everything stays in scope until the takeoff result is returned, thread notified etc. (if you use it perhaps put a timeout or something....).
```cpp
void MyClass::takeoffSync(mavsdk::Action::Result& result)
{
    LOG_INFO("Mavlink::takeoffSync() Method called!");
    std::condition_variable cv;
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    bool done = false;

    // Takeoff is an async function, so we need to wait for it to complete before returning
    action->takeoff_async([&](mavsdk::Action::Result tempResult) { 
        result = tempResult;
        done = true;
        cv.notify_one();
    });
    
    // Wait until the async function has completed...
    cv.wait(lock, [&]{ return done; });
}
```

This one follows the `mavsdk` structure a little more closely with the `future` and `promise` approach. This one will throw errors as you'd expect because of the scope issue mentioned above. You can either do a timer or catch the error now without the seg-fault issue.
```cpp
std::future<mavsdk::Action::Result> MyClass::takeoffAsync()
{
    LOG_INFO("Mavlink::takeoffAsync() Method called!");

    auto sharedPromise = std::make_shared<std::promise<mavsdk::Action::Result>>();
    auto future = sharedPromise->get_future();

    std::atomic<bool> isValueSet{ false };
    action->takeoff_async([sharedPromise, &isValueSet](mavsdk::Action::Result result) {
        if (!isValueSet.exchange(true)) {
            sharedPromise->set_value(result);
        }
    });

   // perhaps add some timer here...

    return future;
}
```

Anyway, @ykhedar I am just confirming that the async approach works for a drone in simulation and for real. I *think* (will check again) that the method still changes it to `GUIDED` though... I am going to try the `takeoff_async_apm` next to see the performance.

In any case, I think the blocking `takeoff` could be fixed with one of my two proposed solutions? Would be good to avoid creating seg-faults in general.

One issue I have with the `STABILIZED` mode in simulation is that the drone will fall out of the sky during the moments when in that mode. This is because it expects a throttle input from somewhere. If there is none, the system drops. See the [flight mode documentation](https://ardupilot.org/plane/docs/flight-modes.html) for what I mean- anything with two `A`'s (e.g. `GUIDED`) means the sim will keep the drone level. Perhaps include this in your dev-team discussions...

